  <p><strong>The federal credit is gone.</strong> Section 25C, the Energy Efficient Home Improvement Credit that paid up to $2,000 toward a qualifying heat pump and up to $600 toward a central AC, expired for property placed in service after December 31, 2025 under the One Big Beautiful Bill Act. A system installed in Wesley Chapel this year does not qualify. If your equipment was installed and running before that date, you may still be able to claim it on your 2025 return, which is a question for your tax preparer rather than for us.</p>
  <p><strong>Utility rebates are still live.</strong> <a href="https://www.duke-energy.com/home/products/home-energy-improvement">Duke Energy Florida's Home Energy Improvement program</a> advertises up to $1,000 back on a qualifying HVAC replacement in a single-family home and up to $600 in a manufactured home. The sequencing is what trips people up: Duke requires a Home Energy Check before the work begins, and you then have 24 months to complete a recommended measure. Book the energy check first, because a rebate claimed after the fact on an unassessed home usually gets denied. Duke sets the amounts and the rules, so confirm current terms on their page before you count on a number, and check the utility name on your power bill, since not every Pasco County address is served by Duke.</p>
  <p><strong>0% financing.</strong> We finance through Synchrony, with promotional 0% APR offers, a decision in most cases within minutes, and no prepayment penalty. The application and the current terms are on our <a href="/financing/">financing page</a>. Plenty of Wesley Chapel homeowners combine the two: the utility rebate reduces the total, the financing spreads what is left.</p>
